.TH __CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_DESTROY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
__ccol_event_loop_destroy \- internal destroy function
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

void __ccol_event_loop_destroy(ccol_event_loop loop);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
loop is an opaque VALUE handle (a packed {slot index, generation}
pair), resolved through a library-owned slot table before the
underlying reactor object is ever touched. loop must be a
currently live handle (one returned by ccol_event_loop_create/
_with_mprocs and not yet destroyed). CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID is a
silent no-op. Any other value (a stale handle already destroyed by
an earlier call or concurrently by another thread, a forged value,
or garbage) is a FATAL ERROR: this function calls ccol_fatal_err()
(abort()/SIGABRT) rather than risk a use-after-free or double-free.

Calling this on loop from within a callback currently dispatching
on one of loop's own threads (the poller thread, or, for
num_reactor_threads > 1, a dispatch worker) is also a FATAL ERROR
(abort()/SIGABRT): that thread is the one this call would otherwise
need to join, and freeing every registration and the loop struct
itself out from under a still-executing callback on that same
thread would be a use-after-free, not merely a deadlock. Defer
destruction to another thread, or to after the callback returns,
instead.

On a live handle called from any other thread, waits for every
in-flight resolved use of the handle to finish (ccol_event_loop_add/
_modify/_pause/_resume/_remove/_reg_count calls currently executing
on another thread), then calls ccol_event_loop_shutdown() if the caller
skipped it (guaranteeing every reactor thread is joined and no
dispatch can be in flight before the next step), then frees every
remaining registration through the same per-registration path
ccol_event_loop_remove() uses.

For queue-backed registrations this correctly unlinks each one from
its queue's own waiter list before freeing it, so a queue that
outlives this ccol_event_loop is never left with a dangling waiter
pointer. fd registrations are not closed and queues are not
destroyed; only the ccol_event_loop's own registration bookkeeping is
freed.

Do not call directly; use the ccol_event_loop_destroy macro, which also
sets the handle to CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR ___ccol_event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_shutdown (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3)
